\section*{Higher bounded Hochschild cohomology of von Neumann algebras}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Every bounded Hochschild cocycle of degree two or higher, with self-coefficients on any complex von Neumann algebra, is claimed to admit an actual bounded primitive, settling Kadison--Ringrose cohomology vanishing. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 295, 2026-09-23): A carefully weighted unitary random walk makes averaging in one cocycle variable harmonic. Free-word configurations and a martingale argument prove that harmonic maps must be right-module maps: distinct multipliers on positive and negative words contradict bounded sine-sum estimates. This module identity constructs primitives, after which classical normal-reduction and type-decomposition results extend the argument to all algebras.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 295, 2026-09-23): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 295, the scope page documents the following selections. The selected bounded Hochschild primitives cover degrees two and above for arbitrary complex von Neumann algebras, without a type or separability condition. The degree-one derivation result is outside this declaration. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: KadisonRingrose.lean. Declarations: OAI.BoundedHochschild.KadisonRingrose.main\_result.

